// EXPECTED: compile error
// expect-error: initializer cannot use @atomic_xchg
// BUG-806 — an atomic is a MEMORY OPERATION on a live address and can never be a
// compile-time constant, but `@atomic_*` was missing from the global-initializer
// rejection list. What the user got instead depended on how many characters the
// name happened to have: the AST atomic branch is gated `nlen >= 10`, so
// `@atomic_or` (9 chars) fell through to the generic "unsupported in constant
// context" marker — loud, and at least NAMED — while `@atomic_xchg` and the five
// `_fetch` forms entered the branch, matched none of its arms, and emitted
// NOTHING: `uint32_t gres = ;`, a C syntax error in generated code the user never
// wrote, reported at a line in a file they never opened.
//
// All fifteen now get the same message at the ZER line. The emitter arm that
// produced the empty string is now unreachable AND loud.
u32 gv = 1;
u32 gres = @atomic_xchg(&gv, 2);

u32 main() { return gres; }
